description.narrative:
-The programme has contributed towards improvement and implementation of policies and legal framework from duty bearers for protection and promotion of women human rights through advocacy and lobbying initiatives at local, national and international level  One example is the acceptance of the request by the Central Election Commission/CEC for the draft law regarding the public register of sexual abusers, after the collection of more than 22,000 signatures from citizens, an initiative led by AWEN and members;

- AWEN and members are becoming increasingly democratic, accountable, independent, sustainable, and capable to promote and protect women human rights. 9 AWEN member organizations have been supported with tailor made and need based capacity building interventions during the whole programme duration; 

- Provision of specialized support services and conducting sensitizing, advocacy, and capacity building initiatives to protect and enhance women human rights at local level, resulting in approximately 9850 girls and women 9 and 240 perpetrators benefitting from the package of services, increasingly more capable local stakeholders in addressing women human right issues and a community that is more supportive and reactive towards women human rights - through the support to AWEN members; 

-  AWEN introduced in 2019, the so-called Emerging Advocacy Grants, which non-members can apply for to implement activities focusing on womens participation in policy and decision-making processes. 41 small scale emergent advocacy interventions have been implemented by non-member organizations, focusing on women economic empowerment, fighting gender stereotypes in media, property rights, awareness raising on GBV and other GE related topics. Through these grants AWEN has managed to increase its thematic and geographic outreach.
